# Cloudflare Pages headers.
#
# IMPORTANT: when several rules match a request, Cloudflare Pages *appends* the
# headers from each one. It does NOT let a later rule override an earlier one.
# So defining Cache-Control both here and in a more specific block produces a
# single concatenated value like
#
#     cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#
# and caches generally honour the FIRST max-age they see — which silently turned
# the year-long asset cache into no caching at all. Set any given header in
# exactly one matching block.
#
# Note these match the REQUEST path, not the file on disk: Astro emits
# dist/about/index.html but it is served at /about/, so a rule like `/*.html`
# would almost never match. Don't reintroduce one.

# Security headers only. Deliberately no Cache-Control here — see above.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), interest-cohort=()

# Astro fingerprints every filename under /_astro/ — CSS, JS and the optimised
# images — so the bytes at a given URL can never change. Cache them forever.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/og.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/rss.xml
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=1800

# HTML intentionally has no rule. Cloudflare Pages already serves it as
# `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which is what we want — the pages are
# rewritten on every deploy and /videos changes on a cron — and adding our own
# rule here would collide with the per-path rules above.

# Sample chapters: not for search indexes, caches, snippets or AI overviews.
# The same directives are in a <meta name="robots"> on those pages; the header
# covers clients that read headers and not markup.
/fiction/:excerpt/
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow, noarchive, nosnippet
